'''Mitsuya Kofun''' (三津屋古墳) is an {{ill|Octagonal Kofun|lt=Octagonal Kofun|ja|八角墳}} located in {{ill|Yoshioka, Gunma|ja|吉岡町}}, Gunma Prefecture. It is designated as a Gunma Prefectural Historic Site.
{{ill|Mitsuya Kofun|ja|三津屋古墳}} is a [[Kofun]] in Japan. Specifically, it is an {{ill|Octagonal Kofun|lt=Octagonal Kofun|ja|八角墳}}.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2015-09-07 |title=Mitsuya Kofun [三津屋古墳] Passage Grave : The Megalithic Portal and Megalith Map |url=https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=31732 |access-date=2023-10-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150907095743/https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=31732 |archive-date=2015-09-07 }}</ref> A rare kind of tomb mostly reserved for 7th century [[Emperor of Japan|Emperors of Japan]].{{sfn|Niiro|1992|p=160}}<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gobyōyama Kofun (in Kyōto) |url=https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=33021 |access-date=2023-10-21}}</ref>[[Emperor Jomei|i]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hays |first=Jeffrey |title=ASUKA, FUJIWARA AND ASUKA-ERA CITIES AND TOMBS {{!}} Facts and Details |url=https://factsanddetails.com/japan/cat16/sub106/entry-5299.html#chapter-16 |access-date=2023-10-20 |website=factsanddetails.com |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":02">{{Cite web |date=2023-10-16 |title=Dan-no-tsuka Kofun [Dannotsuka Kofun 段ノ塚古墳, Jomei Tennō-ryō Jomei Tennnoryo 舒明天皇] Passage Grave : The Megalithic Portal and Megalith Map |url=https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=33044 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016234831/https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=33044 |archive-date=2023-10-16 |access-date=2023-10-20}}</ref>.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Kengoshizuka Kofun Tumulus, Koshitsuka-gomon Kofun Tumulus |url=http://asuka-japan-heritage.jp/global/en/saimei/detail/kengosiduka_sa.html |access-date=2023-10-20 |website=Exploring the Footsteps of the Heroines of Asuka |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite news |date=2022-03-04 |title=Octagonal burial mound said to belong to ancient Japan empress reconstructed in Nara Pref. |language=en |work=Mainichi Daily News |url=https://mainichi.jp/english/articles/20220304/p2a/00m/0na/040000c |access-date=2023-10-20}}</ref>, The inner chamber is open to the public.<ref name=":0" /> unlike many other Kofun which are strictly restricted by the [[Imperial Household Agency]]
[[File:Mitsuya Kofun, funkyu-1.jpg|thumb|the entrance of the Kofun]]


== Overview ==
[[File:Mitsuya Kofun, funkyu from behind.jpg|thumb|250px|right|{{center|Tumulus}}]]
[[File:Mitsuya Kofun, sekishitsu.jpg|thumb|250px|right|{{center|Stone chamber}}]]

Mitsuya Kofun is located on the eastern foothills of {{ill|Mount Haruna|ja|榛名山}}, near the left bank of the Gōōzu River, a tributary of the Tone River. Excavation in 1993 revealed its unique octagonal shape.

The tumulus is two-tiered, constructed using a rammed-earth technique. The outer surface is covered with stones, and a surrounding moat is present. The burial facility features a bilateral entry horizontal stone chamber, with the rear wall forming an octagonal focal point that opens southward. Although the chamber is heavily damaged, it is constructed with natural and dressed stones. The absence of burial goods is attributed to looting.

Mitsuya Kofun dates back to the late {{ill|7th century|ja|7世紀}} and represents the distinctive octagonal tumulus style associated with imperial tombs such as the {{ill|Tennōryō Kofun|ja|天皇陵}}. Its unique design highlights the influence of the Yamato administration in the Kōzuke region.

== Historical Significance ==
* In 1993, an excavation was conducted, with findings published in 1996.
* On March 24, 1995, the kofun was designated a Gunma Prefectural Historic Site.
* The site boundaries were expanded on March 23, 2001.

== Tumulus Features ==
The tumulus measures 23.8 meters diagonally and stands 4.5 meters tall. Its octagonal base measures approximately 9 meters on the lower tier and 6 meters on the upper tier. Stone coverings on the lower tier use mountain stones, while the upper tier features flat river stones.

== Cultural Properties ==
=== Gunma Prefectural Designation ===
* Historic Site
